<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  aps-environment is a build setting, not a literal, and that is the point.

  A TestFlight or App Store build always produces production APNs tokens; an
  Xcode-installed build always produces sandbox tokens. Pinning one value here means
  one of those two paths is wrong, and the only symptom is BadDeviceToken on every
  send — no crash, no log, just silence where a reminder should have been.

  Adjutant pinned it to `development` while shipping through TestFlight. Its push
  works only because an environment variable elsewhere happens to compensate. Deriving
  it from the configuration makes the two impossible to disagree: APS_ENVIRONMENT is
  `development` in Debug and `production` in Release, and PushRegistration.environment
  is derived from the same #if DEBUG, so the token the app registers and the
  entitlement it was signed with always match.

  Deliberately absent: UIBackgroundModes = remote-notification. Syl never makes a
  reminder depend on a silent push — they are throttled to a couple an hour, dropped
  entirely in Low Power Mode or with Background App Refresh off, and Apple's guidance
  is that you may receive none at all. A capability we do not rely on is one we should
  not ask for.

  time-sensitive is the opposite case: a capability we DID rely on and had not asked
  for. `syl-kqc`.

  The Commander set a five minute reminder on 2026-08-10. It was created, scheduled
  and delivered 362ms after its due instant, and he never saw it — Focus mode was on.
  `jobs/deliver-reminders.ts` already sends every `commitment` and every urgent
  reminder with `interruptionLevel: time-sensitive`, which is the level that exists
  to break through Focus. Without the entitlement below, iOS does not reject that
  notification and does not warn: it accepts it and quietly delivers at normal
  priority, which Focus then suppresses. Server says delivered, Apple says accepted,
  the phone shows nothing, and no layer anywhere records an error.

  That is constraint 4 — silently lost, in precisely the situation the feature exists
  for, because Focus is on exactly when an interruption is worth making.

  It is also this project's recurring defect one more time: two things that must agree
  with nothing making one a function of the other. The payload claimed a capability
  the binary was never signed for, and nothing compared them. The test that closes it
  compares every interruption level the server can emit against the levels this file
  permits — see `entitlements-match-payloads`.

  This key alone is not enough. Time Sensitive Notifications must also be enabled on
  the App ID so the provisioning profile carries it, and the Commander must allow it
  for Syl in iOS Settings. A correct build with that toggle off is indistinguishable
  from the bug, which is why all three are named here rather than in a commit message.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>aps-environment</key>
	<string>$(APS_ENVIRONMENT)</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.usernotifications.time-sensitive</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  HealthKit. `syl-t9tj.1.1`.

	  Same three-part shape as time-sensitive above, and it is worth stating that
	  the lesson transferred rather than being learned twice:

	    1. this key, here                              — done
	    2. HealthKit on the App ID, so the provisioning
	       profile carries it                          — done, by the Commander, 2026-08-12
	    3. his per-type grant, at runtime, on the phone — asked for on first read

	  HealthKit's authorisation is ASYMMETRIC in a way that makes a missing step
	  silent, and worse than the time-sensitive case. A type he has not granted
	  does not error and does not report denial — IT READS AS EMPTY. So a build
	  that was never authorised and a Commander who genuinely took no steps are
	  the same answer from here down, forever, with every layer reporting success.

	  That is why `HealthReader` reports what it was AUTHORISED FOR alongside what
	  it read, and why the server refuses to interpret silence it cannot
	  attribute. The entitlement is not the guarantee; the report is.

	  Read-only, deliberately: `NSHealthShareUsageDescription` and no
	  `NSHealthUpdateUsageDescription`. Syl reads his body and never writes to it.
	  A capability we do not rely on is one we should not ask for — the same rule
	  that kept `UIBackgroundModes` out of this file.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.healthkit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
